GLES 2.0 has framebuffer objects in core.

The engine starts, sets up its GL context, and then refuses to run:

    Your graphics card doesn't appear to be fully compatible with OpenGL
    shaders. The game does not support pre-shader graphics cards.
    Location: Device.cpp:340 (Create)

The check behind that message asks, among other things, whether framebuffer
objects exist:

    !ogl_HaveExtension("GL_EXT_framebuffer_object")
        && !ogl_HaveExtension("GL_ARB_framebuffer_object")

FBOs have been part of core GLES since 2.0, and an ES driver never lists them
under their desktop names -- so on a plain ES 2.0 context both come out false
and the game gives up. ogl_HaveExtension does know about extensions that a core
version subsumes, but these two are listed under have_30 only.

Nobody notices on a device: real hardware reports ES 3.x, have_30 is true, and
the desktop names match there. The iOS Simulator's software renderer reports
ES 2.0, and there the check bites.

--- source/lib/ogl.cpp
+++ source/lib/ogl.cpp
@@ -123,6 +123,23 @@
 		MATCH(GL_ARB_point_sprite);
 		MATCH(GL_EXT_blend_equation_separate);
 	}
+#if CONFIG2_GLES
+	if(have_20)
+	{
+		// GLES 2.0 has framebuffer objects in core, and an ES driver never
+		// lists them under their desktop names -- so the renderer's check for
+		// "are there FBOs" comes out false on a plain ES 2.0 context and the
+		// game refuses to start with "your graphics card doesn't appear to be
+		// fully compatible with OpenGL shaders" (Device.cpp).
+		//
+		// It goes unnoticed on real hardware, where the driver reports ES 3.x
+		// and have_30 already matches these names below. The iOS Simulator's
+		// software renderer reports ES 2.0, and there the check bites.
+		MATCH(GL_EXT_framebuffer_object);
+		MATCH(GL_ARB_framebuffer_object);
+	}
+#endif
+
 	if(have_15)
 	{
 		MATCH(GL_ARB_vertex_buffer_object);
